            <ArticleTitle>Chronic Pulmonary Embolism Mimicking Pulmonary Angiosarcoma.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>148</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.2484/rcr.v3i4.148</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We present the case of a 41-year-old woman who presented with chest pain. CT for possible pulmonary embolism showed a mass filling the left main pulmonary artery, with imaging features suggestive of pulmonary angiosarcoma. Open thoracotomy for excision of the mass revelaed no mass, only evidence of a chronic pulmonary thrombus of the left pulmonary artery. We conclude that the radiologic features of chronic pulmonary thrombus may mimick those of pulmonary angiosarcoma.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
